WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.664
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.664 --> 00:00:10.016
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:10.016 --> 00:00:14.790
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.390
&gt;&gt; Say, Sir Ronald, welcome.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.100
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.340
&gt;&gt; We are delighted to have
you with us here today.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.710
And so there's many budding entrepreneur
here in the audience who would be quite

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.810
satisfied if they had the one
successful venture under their belt.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.830
But you've not only successfully co
founded five to date and counting but

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.310
also established multiple
sectors along the way.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.330
But this wasn't without
encountering many a skeptic.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.670
So can talk us through how you calibrate
the extent to which you should have

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.510
courage of your own convictions and ignore
the naysayers versus engage them and

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.480
take heed of their warning.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.320
&gt;&gt; So the best way is to start young.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.140
&gt;&gt; Yes. I'm naive. &gt;&gt; And
then think big and then stick with it.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.890
I think with advancing years
you become more skeptical.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:03.960
Some become cynical.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:09.830
And my experience has been in developing
venture capital, and then private equity,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.190
and now impact investment.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.170
That you should focus on those
who are enthusiastic, first,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.560
who get it, don't need convincing.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.360
Then there's those who are skeptical,
and you should just avoid the cynics.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.210
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.740
&gt;&gt; Because those who believe that nothing
new can be done or that nothing should be

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.230
done for the first time
are plentiful in our society.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:36.580
If any of you've been involved
in impact investment and

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.178
you know that this isn't something
that everybody immediately picks up.

00:01:41.178 --> 00:01:45.510
Never the less it's the future.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:50.420
And I've just come from Mexico
in Merida which is an hour and

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.680
a half away from Mexico City.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.660
400 people gathered from 20
Latin American countries to talk about

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.540
impact investment, and
that's just one indication.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:06.330
I was in Brazil and 600 people came
to a meeting on impact investment.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:12.050
When you meet with government officials
you find people are aware of the fact that

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.640
society can not continue to tackle social
issues in the way that we are doing.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.810
Social for me includes environmental.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.210
So we've come a long way since 2000.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:29.100
When you find an idea that makes sense,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:36.640
you think of it becoming global,
you begin to attract inspired people.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.920
When you think small and
deal with the skeptics,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.280
you don't tend to attract inspired people.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.832
And the people who are flocking, it's not
an exact duration to impact investment and

00:02:49.832 --> 00:02:53.510
now, are the best and the brightest.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:54.320
You're an example.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.820
&gt;&gt; That's very kind of you.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.477
I paid him earlier to say that.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:59.705
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But on the point of skeptics,

00:02:59.705 --> 00:03:03.020
some critics of the valley,
that accuse it of being too insular,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.690
preferring specialists,
and lacking in diversity.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:11.470
Given that you successfully transferred
ideas between geographies and

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.000
between sectors with big society and
social finance.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.210
To what extent do you encourage
the students here to be polymaths,

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.970
take the scenic route and
have a multifaceted career,

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.390
versus kind of playing to their
strengths early on and focusing?

00:03:26.958 --> 00:03:30.940
&gt;&gt; So could I answer that by just
going through some of my history?

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.150
&gt;&gt; By all means.
&gt;&gt; And then I'll explain,

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:39.130
perhaps I'll answer your question directly
&gt;&gt; So the reason I got into venture

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.180
capital is that a speaker
came to the business school,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.780
General who had been professor
of entrepreneurship,

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.328
I guess you'd call it today, at
Harvard Business School, the other place.

00:03:51.328 --> 00:03:56.760
And General unlikely figure.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:02.490
You might think a French military leader
was one of the first venture capitalists.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:09.420
He invested in and the discussion rather
as I'm speaking to all of you today.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.030
He said look I put $70,000 that
which was digital equipment company,

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:20.480
and it's going public and
my stake's going to be worth $100 million.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.900
That caught my attention.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.210
That's new and interesting.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:33.370
The time when I was at business school,
the plum jobs were working for

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:39.290
IBM or any of the big, General Motors,
any of the big corporations.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.540
Small was not yet beautiful.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:45.070
But you felt that
something was in the air.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:51.890
Intel had just been created,
there was a buzz around semi conductors.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:56.600
And I felt at that time as I feel today.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:00.630
That there was something in the air
that was going to be major.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.520
And I decided to go into venture capital.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.810
Now it was a hard slog,
venture capital, in those days.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.860
In the UK people couldn't spell
the word entrepreneur let alone

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:13.770
the word venture capitalists.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.200
&gt;&gt; Well, it's French, so you know.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.430
&gt;&gt; Sorry?
&gt;&gt; It's French.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.160
&gt;&gt; It's French.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.990
&gt;&gt; So we try to ignore it.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:25.240
&gt;&gt; And this is a true story, but one of my
venture capitalist friends took his kid

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:31.710
to interview in a school, for a position
in the school, to join the school.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:37.780
And the headmaster asked the son, said
you know sonny what does your father do?

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.918
He said, he's an adventure copulist.

00:05:39.918 --> 00:05:45.594
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:45.594 --> 00:05:49.850
&gt;&gt; So, it was brand new.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:51.740
You couldn't even spell it.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.585
Even though some kids had
innovative minds, as you can see.

00:05:55.585 --> 00:06:00.440
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I decided at that time,

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.010
look this is big enough and

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:08.430
important enough that I left
Mackenzie after just two years.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.790
I'm going to make a go of this.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.080
And I think this is the future.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.620
Now, if you connect with something which
you feel is going to be there then you

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.990
don't need a very long run up for it.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:28.159
I don't need to say that in Silicon Valley
which has led the way in young

00:06:28.159 --> 00:06:33.180
entrepreneurs transforming the world.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.280
And I think when one
talks in terms of should

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.730
one devote one's time
to impact investment,

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:44.900
we'll talk about the two strands of
impact investment which are developing.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:49.780
The one which has to do with
the funding of not-for-profits,

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:55.620
and the one that has to do with the
funding of profit with purpose companies.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.850
If you can connect with something that you
think has real potential in these areas,

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:04.920
then you should connect with it and
get on with it.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.680
If you're unsure about whether or not
you've connected with something like that,

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.690
then you're better off
pursuing your career and

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.170
acquiring more experience,
until you do that.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:18.990
And even in the not-for-profit sector
where today a social organization can

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:23.160
build a balance sheet in the same
way that the business can.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.580
That's what impact investment has done.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:29.320
It's provided layers of capital for
not-for-profits.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.350
The purpose of it was to connect social
entrepreneurs to the capital markets.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:35.990
And we've done that.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:41.390
It's an evolution in the idea that we've
got to do more than just make money.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:47.356
But it's a revolution in the means that
we have brought to not-for-profits

00:07:47.356 --> 00:07:52.698
seeking to scale so that they can
tackle social issues effectively.

00:07:52.698 --> 00:07:57.177
And if previously we have
all assumed that nothing

00:07:57.177 --> 00:08:01.643
can be measured in the social area
&gt;&gt; And of course,

00:08:01.643 --> 00:08:08.790
that's true in the sense of valuing
an improvement in a prisoner's life or

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:13.215
an improvement in
a homeless person's life or

00:08:13.215 --> 00:08:18.102
an improvement in a diabetic or
prediabetic life.

00:08:18.102 --> 00:08:22.819
Any of the 12 social issues that
are now being addressed by the 55

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:26.310
social impact bonds,
which have been launched.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:30.978
You can't value that, but you can measure
in percentage terms and improvement

00:08:30.978 --> 00:08:36.250
verses the past or verses a control
group of people who haven't been helped.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:39.676
And when you link that
to a financial return,

00:08:39.676 --> 00:08:44.455
which represents only a fraction
of the saving to government,

00:08:44.455 --> 00:08:50.605
usually in the first year, then you have
something that is extremely powerful.

00:08:50.605 --> 00:08:56.051
You begin to have the tools to build
an organization that's motivated to help

00:08:56.051 --> 00:09:01.600
others in the same way that you can an
organization that is there to make money.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.309
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, on this idea of inclusive

00:09:04.309 --> 00:09:09.762
capitalism and this idea around engaging
the invisible heart of the markets

00:09:09.762 --> 00:09:14.890
to create opportunities for
those the invisible hand has left behind.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:20.883
You're clearly a proponent of this is
the market solving these social issues,

00:09:20.883 --> 00:09:25.980
but are there any areas whereby you
think markets actually can't so

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:27.603
full and why is that?

00:09:27.603 --> 00:09:32.560
&gt;&gt; So, the answer is that not everything
can be measured in the way that I

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.852
have described.

00:09:33.852 --> 00:09:36.183
There are lots of social issues,

00:09:36.183 --> 00:09:41.480
which will continue to be tackled
by not for profits through grants.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.540
We're not trying to displace grants.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:49.250
What we're trying to do, Nieve is we're
trying to do what we did for business

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:56.251
in areas which improve society and
the planet.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:03.160
We're trying to connect social
entrepreneurs to capital,

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:08.767
which enables them to harness
entrepreneurship and innovation.

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:13.567
And when in 2000,
I was asked by the Treasury as Charles

00:10:13.567 --> 00:10:18.271
mentioned to look at the question
of poverty in Britain and

00:10:18.271 --> 00:10:23.267
I started to look at how society
has dealt with social issues.

00:10:23.267 --> 00:10:26.102
I discovered some astonishing things.

00:10:26.102 --> 00:10:30.826
I discovered that really philanthropists
were the first to tackle social

00:10:30.826 --> 00:10:32.850
issues not governments.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:37.333
In the 19th century, they began to
organize themselves as foundations,

00:10:37.333 --> 00:10:41.674
which got tax breaks at the beginning
of the 20th century when all the big

00:10:41.674 --> 00:10:44.351
foundations or
many of the big foundations.

00:10:44.351 --> 00:10:47.340
Now there are much bigger
ones being set up, but

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:51.376
many of the household name foundations,
The Ford Foundation,

00:10:51.376 --> 00:10:57.550
Rockefeller Foundation in the mid 30s, the
Wilcom Foundation in the UK were set up.

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:02.100
By the 1930s, governments were realizing

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.070
that philanthropists couldn't
cope with social issues.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:10.140
Very soon,
were being left behind as economic

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:14.540
growth propelled most of us forward.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.700
Needed help from the government and

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:22.630
governments started to get involved
directly in improving lives.

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:28.260
By the 1940s, we came to the welfare
state, which took on the responsibility

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:34.930
for creating this safety net for
citizens who were more vulnerable.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:37.440
And today, welfare states
are throwing their hands up.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:41.360
Across the world, they look at the gap,

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.790
a yawning gap between
the provision of social services.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:51.150
Their ability to pay for
that and likely social needs

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:57.760
with an aging population where the aging
pool is a real challenge in the future.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.726
And so, the idea came to us then on
the Social Investment Task Force.

00:12:04.726 --> 00:12:10.101
Venture capital was a response to
the needs of tech entrepreneurs.

00:12:10.101 --> 00:12:15.009
It was a major innovation
creating ten year

00:12:15.009 --> 00:12:19.644
illiquid funds in order to fund dropouts

00:12:19.644 --> 00:12:24.143
with vision was really a new concept and

00:12:24.143 --> 00:12:29.732
why can't we find a similar
response to the needs

00:12:29.732 --> 00:12:37.809
of social entrepreneurs who
are motivated by empathy to help others?

00:12:37.809 --> 00:12:41.559
And I felt, as did my colleagues on
the Social Investment Task Force.

00:12:41.559 --> 00:12:45.630
So the way to do that was to
create a social investment bank.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:49.990
That would bring together people
from the financial world and

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:56.940
people from the social world, and
innovate in making that connection.

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:02.809
Well, if you fast forward that
you've known me ten years.

00:13:02.809 --> 00:13:05.475
In 2010,
two young people at Social Finance,

00:13:05.475 --> 00:13:09.018
which was founded in the basement
of my office in Portsmouth Place.

00:13:09.018 --> 00:13:13.184
We don't have garages for
startups in the UK, we have basements.

00:13:13.184 --> 00:13:16.361
It has the advantage that the only
way out of the basement is up.

00:13:16.361 --> 00:13:16.976
&gt;&gt; Up.
[LAUGH]

00:13:16.976 --> 00:13:20.602
&gt;&gt; So, an increased level of motivation.

00:13:20.602 --> 00:13:25.669
And these two young people came to me and
said, we were talking to people

00:13:25.669 --> 00:13:30.653
in Birmingham in the UK about
prisoner reoffending more than 60% of

00:13:30.653 --> 00:13:36.920
prisoners incarcerated before the age
of 21 in UK and in every other country.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:42.090
Go back to prison within 18 months and
we came up with the idea of

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:47.770
connecting a reduction in the percentage
that goes back to a financial return.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.602
I said, wow.

00:13:51.602 --> 00:13:56.419
That is the key to the capital markets for
social entrepreneurs and

00:13:56.419 --> 00:14:01.493
then as we began to work on it with
the Ministry of Justice and we started

00:14:01.493 --> 00:14:06.912
pushing numbers, we began to realize
that the cost of the intervention and

00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:11.143
it's one of the essential
drivers of impact investment.

00:14:11.143 --> 00:14:17.670
The cost of prevention is a fraction
of the saving of that government makes.

00:14:19.158 --> 00:14:23.640
And so, we created the first
social impact bonds in

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:29.190
2010 in just three months and

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:35.340
we found ourselves creating a revolution
in the way that prisoners are dealt with.

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:41.835
Does it occur to any of us
that prison management across

00:14:41.835 --> 00:14:47.559
the world has no responsibility for
the reoffending rate?

00:14:47.559 --> 00:14:51.490
It's unbelievable,
prison management has responsibility for

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:55.142
the cost per prisoner and
the number of incidence in prison.

00:14:55.142 --> 00:14:57.102
It's unbelievable.

00:14:57.102 --> 00:15:02.288
And when you look at the way
the social sector, the not for

00:15:02.288 --> 00:15:07.059
profits help prisoners,
which is what we had to do.

00:15:07.059 --> 00:15:11.555
We raised 5 million pounds to fund for
not for profits,

00:15:11.555 --> 00:15:16.054
you realize that not for
profits have grown haphazardly

00:15:16.054 --> 00:15:21.142
dealing with a specific bit of
the train to achieve an outcome.

00:15:21.142 --> 00:15:26.227
To simplify, one not for
profit is helping prisoners in prison.

00:15:26.227 --> 00:15:27.809
One's helping them at the gate.

00:15:27.809 --> 00:15:29.580
One's helping them get into a job.

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:30.810
One's helping them stay in a job.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:34.726
No one has responsibility for
achieving the outcome.

00:15:34.726 --> 00:15:38.608
And when you begin to get
5 million pounds to say,

00:15:38.608 --> 00:15:41.849
let's reduce the rate by 7.5 % plus.

00:15:41.849 --> 00:15:47.977
because below 7.5%, the social impact bond
doesn't pay back, doesn't get paid back.

00:15:47.977 --> 00:15:49.854
It's just a philanthropic donation.

00:15:49.854 --> 00:15:55.355
Let's get a reduction between 7.5 and
a 15% reduction,

00:15:55.355 --> 00:15:59.208
so that our investors can make 2 to 13%.

00:15:59.208 --> 00:16:02.728
A return on their investment.

00:16:02.728 --> 00:16:06.801
You begin to focus on developing
the necessary information,

00:16:06.801 --> 00:16:11.736
in the same way that management by
objectives 40 years ago got businesses

00:16:11.736 --> 00:16:15.490
to create dashboards and
key performance indicators.

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:17.510
You begin to do the same.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:23.580
You begin to push for understanding

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:28.400
how you deal with the accommodation
problem that 40% of prisoners face,

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:33.398
they don't know where they
are going to spend the night.

00:16:33.398 --> 00:16:38.030
The 18% that still have a drug habit,
the 25%

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:42.280
that has insufficient money to make it
through to the first government pay check.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:48.480
And so what's happening is a prisoner
gets to the prison gates with those three

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.060
issues and the drug dealer meets them
at the gate and says come, I'll give

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:56.080
you a place to sleep and something
to forget what you've been through.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:59.850
And they're back in,
two-thirds of them are back in prison.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:05.040
I take this example because
the cost of intervention

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:10.070
is probably about a third of a saving
the government makes in the first year.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:15.708
The investors in the first social impact
bond will make 7%, the government who's

00:17:15.708 --> 00:17:20.210
changed the whole of the probation office,
not necessarily in the best way possible,

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:25.129
but probably as a result
of the focus on prisoners.

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:30.280
Which, and prisoner rehabilitation,
which the social impact bond brought.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:38.130
And so when you look at 55 social impact
bonds today across the world, across

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:44.008
a dozen countries, two were announced in
the last few days in the United States.

00:17:44.008 --> 00:17:49.130
A $30 million South Carolina
social impact bond

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:54.800
to prevent teenage pregnancies,
or reduce teenage pregnancies.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:59.250
A $12 and a half million social
impact bond in Connecticut

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:03.270
dealing with unstable families
plagued by drug abuse.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:09.530
When you begin to look at how homelessness
is being tackled, how primary drop

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:16.010
out rates from primary school in India is
being tackled, how drop out rates from

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:22.145
computer studies at college level
in Israel is being tackled.

00:18:22.145 --> 00:18:26.245
When you look at unemployed
youth in the UK,

00:18:26.245 --> 00:18:30.715
and the way that social impact bonds
have funded organizations that help them

00:18:30.715 --> 00:18:35.735
acquire the skills to get into jobs,
you begin to feel that this is

00:18:35.735 --> 00:18:41.770
really bringing about a revolution in
the way that we tackle social issues.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:46.720
But as you know, Maeve,
it's only the beginning of the revolution.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:47.800
&gt;&gt; And on that point, so

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:51.330
how do you see the sector as
a whole evolving going forward?

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.490
What are the new models that are emerging?

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:57.090
And who are the key players
that are driving change today?

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:01.583
The next Bill yourself?

00:19:01.583 --> 00:19:07.490
&gt;&gt; So, the social impact bond brought
us to the realization that measurement

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:13.220
is actually central to tackling social and
environmental issues.

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:20.690
And that you can connect it to a financial
return and access the capital markets.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:25.570
There are investors who will say, wow 7%

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:31.110
uncorrelated return with the stock markets
and interest rates, because you don't

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:36.140
know what the bond is going to pay until
later in it's life, that's pretty cool.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:37.220
People would put five,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:42.730
ten percent of their portfolio in
absolute return instruments of this kind.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:49.068
But when we created the G8 task force,
and we had 200 people

00:19:49.068 --> 00:19:53.880
across the world involved through
national advisory boards,

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:59.030
Matt Banick of Medea was one of
my close partners in this effort.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:03.800
And people like Matt and
Tracey Polantron of social finance,

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:08.700
who together led the National
Advisory Board in the United States.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:12.700
People like that across the G7
countries plus Australia

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:21.640
brought all of their know-how about social
issues and finance to the task force.

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:26.630
And we realized that something much
more fundamental was happening than just

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.798
innovating with an instrument.

00:20:28.798 --> 00:20:34.820
We realized then that
the world had shifted,

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:39.800
if in the 19th century the world
measured financial return,

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:44.268
Adam Smith and before,
all the way through to the 19th century.

00:20:44.268 --> 00:20:49.820
In the 20th century, we have innovated
by measuring risk and return and

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:54.710
by opening the window on
risk we created a need for

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:57.290
new concepts like diversification.

00:20:57.290 --> 00:21:04.050
Which led to the notion which
the endowments lead, including Stanford,

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:08.760
that you can include in a diversified
portfolio high risk assets.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:13.000
Which may be a bit more volatile but
you yield much better returns and

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:18.020
improve the return on your portfolio,
then in

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:23.060
the 21st century we were already
measuring risk return and impact.

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:26.550
Only people haven't realized it fully.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:32.300
Those who are unpopular today,
be they in the financial services area,

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:37.520
or utilities like electricity
generation companies,

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.840
are unpopular because they're perceived
as having negative impact on society.

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:49.170
A utility per se is not an unpopular
company but the utility

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:55.270
which is deemed to charge its customers
more than it should if they are poor and

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:59.930
to account for a big percentage of their
disposable income is judged negatively.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:04.640
And when you begin to measure
impact alongside risk and

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:10.620
return you open the window again
on a new set of opportunities.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.430
Themes in the profit with purpose area,
for instance.

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.830
Such as The Gym in the UK or

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:22.750
Tom Shoes in the United States or
Warby Parker or Esty.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:29.120
When my colleagues at Bridges started to
look at how can we improve the health

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:33.980
of people living in poor areas
the answer was we should create gyms.

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:39.581
But if you create a gym in a poor
area it has to be open 24/7.

00:22:39.581 --> 00:22:44.417
It has to be large, because you’re
going to charge a quarter of

00:22:44.417 --> 00:22:48.082
what you can charge in
the main stream economy.

00:22:48.082 --> 00:22:52.690
So it's a 15,000 square foot gym.

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:59.000
No membership fee and no frills,
no changing rooms, no jacuzzi, no spa.

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:07.110
The gym floated for 250 million pounds
just a few months ago in the UK.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:12.590
Several thousand members,
are joining before the gym opens.

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:17.560
30 to 40% of the members have never
been able to afford a gym before.

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.370
So what's the window you're opening?

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:24.580
You're opening a window on themes,
that say, if I come up with a product or

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:33.690
a service at a low cost, which is capable
of satisfying huge latent demand.

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.720
I can grow it faster than
a mainstream business.

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:43.743
And so, what is beginning to happen
today is that the measurement of risk,

00:23:43.743 --> 00:23:48.373
return, and impact is beginning
to lead to a revolution in

00:23:48.373 --> 00:23:53.678
the types of business models that
entrepreneurs are developing.

00:23:53.678 --> 00:23:58.038
And these two streams,
the funding of not for

00:23:58.038 --> 00:24:03.434
profits to achieve social
goals Which works better when

00:24:03.434 --> 00:24:08.485
the communities help is
required in rehabilitating

00:24:08.485 --> 00:24:13.550
a prisoner or absorbing a homeless person.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:18.430
And the other stream of profit with
purpose, which works better where you're

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:23.400
developing products and
services that can generate cash flow and

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:27.190
take advantage of high growth.

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:30.690
Those two streams of coming together
at the same time as social and

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:32.300
environmental coming together.

00:24:33.442 --> 00:24:39.350
And we're beginning to see
governments across the world saying,

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:43.548
we can't cope,
we need money from the capital markets.

00:24:43.548 --> 00:24:46.530
It's entrepreneurs who're
going to come with innovation and

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:48.620
how we tackle social issues.

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:53.810
Who are going to fund prevention
that we need to bring in.

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:57.790
And so country by country we're
beginning to see governments

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:00.110
introducing incentives for this.

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:04.010
&gt;&gt; So regarding governments,
by their very nature they're transitional

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:07.510
both in terms of the stakeholders and
the agenda.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:12.420
So how do you ensure that you
manage to maintain momentum and

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:16.850
push your initiatives through to
completion given all the ongoing change?

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:21.050
&gt;&gt; Well that's the great strength of
instruments like social impact bonds.

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:28.990
Because as Iain Duncan Smith, one of our
senior ministers in government today,

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:33.890
in charge of the department of works and
pensions puts it, when you sign up for

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:38.510
a social impact bond, you get what's
written on the tin as he puts it.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:43.360
The way he expresses it is,
look, I come in as a minister.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:45.110
I have great intentions.

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:46.700
I want to make something happen.

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:48.890
We develop the idea.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:55.730
Some of my officials who always wanted to
do something specific tried to add it in.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:59.660
Some of my officials who
hate something take it out.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:02.080
And then there's an election and
I'm gone and

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:07.290
the program continues badly designed for
decades and nothing much comes from it.

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:11.770
With the social impact bond, when you
sign a contract with the investors to put

00:26:11.770 --> 00:26:16.530
money up on the basis of which
there are social metrics

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:22.760
which are going to mean that
your performance is objectively

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:27.540
audited and you're paid out according
to that, you can't deviate from it.

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:29.930
It has to run the seven years,
or the five years, or

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:33.820
the ten years of the social impact bond.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:38.690
And it's the way for
us to create benchmarks and

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:42.220
innovate in the way that
we tackle social issues so

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:45.790
as to improve the delivery
of government services.

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:48.170
It's not to substitute for government.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:54.860
It's an instrument where government,
most of the time, pays out on results.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.900
Sometimes foundations
will pay out on results.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:01.430
Sometimes health management
organizations will pay out.

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:07.040
Sometimes universities will pay out as
in the Computer studies dropout example.

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:15.080
But fundamentally, you're innovating
in the way we tackle social issues and

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.840
you're setting benchmarks
which government then

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:20.930
can take into account in
formulating it's own policy.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:21.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:26.650
Some of the issues today,
sadly, are just so complex.

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:29.570
The current refugee
crisis being one of them.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:32.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Now, given all of your success to date,

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:37.940
many here may not know how you arose to be
in the UK to go on to Oxford and beyond.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:41.600
And this I think the story is
especially poignant with its

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:43.070
backdrop of the refugee crisis.

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:44.380
So I was wondering if you'd be so

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:48.260
kind as to kindly share with us
as why your family fled Egypt.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:53.050
And what it was like as an 11
year boy arriving in Britain,

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:57.025
speaking no English, ten pounds in
the pocket and just a suitcase each.

00:27:57.025 --> 00:27:58.054
Sorry.

00:27:58.054 --> 00:28:01.934
&gt;&gt; Well, it was interesting.

00:28:01.934 --> 00:28:05.710
[LAUGH] I had my stamp
collection under my arm.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:10.270
And I was very worried that it would be
taken away from me as I was leaving Egypt.

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:15.570
I have very good memories of
Egypt as a very liberal place.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:20.280
I went to a Catholic school
where on Fridays you could go to

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:26.120
the Catholic service or
the Muslim service or the Jewish service.

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:31.180
It was the Friars of St. John the Baptist,
one of the best schools in Egypt.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:36.820
And the reason that we were kicked out
of Egypt is there was something called

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:42.440
the Suez Crisis, which might seem like
the dim and distant past to many of you.

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.857
It occurred in 1956.

00:28:44.857 --> 00:28:49.775
When Nasser nationalized, Nasser who
was then the president of Egypt,

00:28:49.775 --> 00:28:52.590
nationalized the Suez Canal.

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:57.770
And Britain, which owned the canal,
France, which built it, and Israel,

00:28:57.770 --> 00:29:02.760
which felt threatened by Nasser,
united to take the canal out.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:08.219
And the United States,
under pressure from Russia,

00:29:08.219 --> 00:29:12.480
intervened and stopped that operation.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:18.540
And Nasser retaliated by expelling
all British and French citizens.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.890
And made it very uncomfortable for
Jews to stay, basically.

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:27.780
And if you were an Egyptian Jew,
as I was and

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:33.150
my family was, then when you left,
you lost your Egyptian nationality.

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:40.020
And there were only three places that
would take you as a stateless citizen.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:44.220
Brazil, Canada, and Australia.

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:49.070
But my mother had a British
passport by quirk of fate.

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:51.800
And we were on our way to Rio de Janeiro.

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:55.720
And we stopped in Milan to catch
the boat in Genoa, to Rio de Janeiro.

00:29:55.720 --> 00:29:58.040
And my mother said I'm going to my dad,

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:00.570
I'm going to, he couldn't travel
because he was stateless.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:05.210
Said I'm going to take the kids and go to
London, and say goodbye to my brothers.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:11.510
And when she got to London, she found my
dad and said, we're staying right here.

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:15.020
This is where you're going to
get the best education for

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:18.000
the kids, this is the place to be.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:21.910
What are we going to go and
do in Brazil miles away from Europe and so

00:30:21.910 --> 00:30:22.440
on and do forth?

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.720
My dad spoke English.

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:31.013
Happily agreed, it was difficult for him
to disagree, with my mother who was a very

00:30:31.013 --> 00:30:37.110
forceful person and so we became British.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:40.675
&gt;&gt; But
we're delighted to have adopted you.

00:30:40.675 --> 00:30:42.800
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I've been,

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:47.010
I've been blessed, to be British.

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:51.240
And Britain has been extremely good to me.

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:54.720
And one interesting quirk,
is that the Henry Fellowship,

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.970
which I got to
Harvard Business School from Oxford,

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:05.520
obligated me to come back to
Britain with something of value.

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:10.000
And the thing I came back with was venture
capital from Harvard Business School.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.830
So these things matter.

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:15.580
So what was it like?

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:18.310
Well my parents turned
it into a challenge.

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:23.240
My dad was very sad to
lose everything in Egypt.

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:26.555
My mother, who was more down to Earth,

00:31:26.555 --> 00:31:31.277
thought Egypt was going to have
a very difficult time, and

00:31:31.277 --> 00:31:36.717
that this was a blessing in disguise,
which it turned out to be.

00:31:36.717 --> 00:31:42.368
So, I viewed it as a challenge, and
I happened to be a good student,

00:31:42.368 --> 00:31:49.330
and actually the education I'd had in this
Catholic school stood me in good stead.

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:54.279
And so, I made it into Oxford
because of one teacher.

00:31:54.279 --> 00:31:55.278
&gt;&gt; A history teacher.

00:31:55.278 --> 00:31:56.018
&gt;&gt; Richard Farley.

00:31:56.018 --> 00:31:59.698
&gt;&gt; Richard Farley, a history teacher.

00:31:59.698 --> 00:32:04.290
Who said to me, Cohen,
you should be going to Oxford.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.770
And he prepared me for the entrance exams.

00:32:06.770 --> 00:32:10.470
In those days you had
a series of entrance exams.

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:15.110
That school had only sent
one person to Oxford before.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:19.260
And Richard Folly trained a few of us,
and four of us got into Oxford.

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:21.160
&gt;&gt; It's a real life case
of The History Boys.

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:22.330
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:32:22.330 --> 00:32:26.740
And then I look for
Richard Farley for years, and

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:32.870
I found him 40 years after I'd
left Orange Hill Grammar School

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:35.380
because I remembered he'd
written a book on education.

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:38.940
And that his publisher
must have his address, and

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:40.560
sure enough, that was the case.

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:45.350
And so I was privileged to reconnect
with him and visit him once or

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:51.490
twice a year, and the most wonderful,
just the most wonderful man.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:56.620
So, when you talk of the immigrant crisis,
I have empathy.

00:32:56.620 --> 00:33:00.090
Not that I had to go
through the tribulations

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:04.990
of the Syrian immigrants that we see
today, by the way, another quirk of fate.

00:33:04.990 --> 00:33:09.700
My grandparents were born in Aleppo,
in Syria and

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:14.590
came down to Egypt when the Suez Canal was
opened because the caravan routes going

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:20.300
through Aleppo died, and all the merchants
went into the Lebanon or to Egypt.

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:29.280
And I think that we have an obligation
to deal with displaced people.

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:33.730
Not just because we don't have
the choice of ignoring it.

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:38.540
You can't have millions of
people spreading through

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:43.700
society not being looked after without
terrible implications for society.

00:33:43.700 --> 00:33:49.760
But I think, it's a moral obligation
we have to help those in need.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:55.600
And one of the efforts I'm making
is to create at central level

00:33:55.600 --> 00:34:03.930
in the EU a billion dollar fund to pay out
on social impact bonds which are going

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:09.370
to fund o organizations that can
help the absorption of immigrants.

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:11.880
&gt;&gt; That's wonderful.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:15.700
Another body that you've
greatly support and

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:21.430
trust which seeks to encourage
private sector in both Israel and

00:34:21.430 --> 00:34:24.150
Palestine as a means to
peace in the Middle East.

00:34:24.150 --> 00:34:26.790
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; Can you talk us through

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:29.770
how business can or
should help resolve conflict?

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:35.660
&gt;&gt; So, there are two people in
the audience, Afir Sampson and

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:36.980
Travis Hollingsworth.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:41.610
Afir worked with me in Israel
at the Portland Trust and

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.700
Travis worked with me
at Big Society Capital.

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:51.270
And I think you know they are examples

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:59.630
of people who feel that one can make a
difference by following these approaches.

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:05.890
And that you can do good and
do well at the same time.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:13.390
The innovation that is coming now is
coming because there are very powerful

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:17.940
drivers as I have said, governments
on the one hand don't have the money.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:21.940
The millennial generation,
of which you're a part,

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:25.190
that feels that it wants to
do more than just make money.

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:32.080
And investors who to the tune
of $55 trillion, have

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:37.340
signed up to the United Nations Principles
of Responsible Investments saying,

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:43.760
we owe it to generate more
than just financial returns.

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:46.320
And social sector organizations, not for

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:51.180
profits, that say to
themselves we want to grow.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:55.832
If we're helping 700 homeless people
in the UK we'd like to help 7,000 or

00:35:55.832 --> 00:36:01.840
30,000 or 50,000 homeless people
at any given time in the UK.

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:05.490
These are powerful drivers, and

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:10.820
I think the fundamental change
that has come is to say,

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:16.200
the allocation of resources
in financial terms,

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:20.500
in terms of the human capital that
you all represent here today,

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:23.730
cannot come just on the basis of risk and
return.

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:26.990
I think being a business that's in it for

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:30.710
just money is not going to
be a popular business.

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:35.219
Already, if you want to attract
the greatest talent in places like

00:36:35.219 --> 00:36:38.200
McKenzie and elsewhere,
you have to elevate the goals.

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:42.984
You have to give the opportunity
to entrance to do

00:36:42.984 --> 00:36:47.850
things that are going to improve society.

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:52.110
And so the transformation that is
going to come through business,

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:57.140
is going to start as the tech
revolution did, is going to start

00:36:57.140 --> 00:37:03.530
with the destructors who are young, and
who say, I'm going to create the business

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:07.530
which is going to deliver the best
combination of risk, return, and impact.

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:13.670
And I'm going to attract the most talented
people because it's a more inspiring goal.

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:19.730
And it'll pull behind it,
just tech did the big corporates,

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:22.870
some of which are reacting today.

00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:26.828
I was at the launch 125 million euro

00:37:26.828 --> 00:37:32.170
impact fund,
launched by the Mars Corporation and

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:36.700
the Danone Food Corporation in France,
two huge food corporations.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:41.820
To take a million people in their
supply chain out of poverty.

00:37:43.880 --> 00:37:52.420
Unilever is beginning to publish a set of
social objectives for itself as a company.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:54.830
And big companies will follow.

00:37:54.830 --> 00:37:58.160
If you look ahead 20 years,

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:03.290
I know Einstein said, the future is
unknowable, but it's not unthinkable.

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:09.490
So let's think,
as we think together look ahead 20 years.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:14.670
We can envisage impact public equities,
impact fixed income,

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:18.730
impact venture capital,
impact private equity,

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:22.160
impact real estate, and
impact absolute return.

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:24.810
Where will the money go?

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:31.495
If investors then have the choice between
a company that delivers a 20 percent

00:38:31.495 --> 00:38:37.487
IRA as an investment, and another company
that delivers a 20 percent IRA and

00:38:37.487 --> 00:38:42.320
a 35 percent social return,
measured because governments publish

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:47.420
as the UK is already doing the cost
of social issues on the web.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:53.970
You go to the cabinet office website, you
will find the cost of 640 social issues.

00:38:53.970 --> 00:38:57.580
You'll be able to work out that the young
prisoner I was talking about is costing

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:00.410
the government 22,000 pounds a year.

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:04.500
And you rehabilitate
1,000 it's 22 million.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:08.048
And 22 million on a 5 million social
impact bond over seven years is

00:39:08.048 --> 00:39:11.780
more than 35% IRA rule, or whatever.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:13.690
Where'd you think the money's going to go?

00:39:13.690 --> 00:39:17.790
Is it going to go to those who are just
building a business to make money?

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:22.570
Or is it going to go to those who
are able to deliver a social return,

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:24.262
or an environmental return?

00:39:24.262 --> 00:39:31.160
At the same time, so that's why we called

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:35.320
our report of the G8 task force
the The Invisible Heart of Markets.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:40.316
Because the Invisible Heart of Markets
is the measurement of impact, and

00:39:40.316 --> 00:39:42.316
it will guide their invisible hand.

00:39:42.316 --> 00:39:47.244
And there's a wonderful, historic element,

00:39:47.244 --> 00:39:52.293
and it is that Adam Smith
was prouder of his book,

00:39:52.293 --> 00:39:55.741
The Theory of Moral Sentiments,

00:39:55.741 --> 00:40:00.933
which is all about why
People help out of empathy.

00:40:00.933 --> 00:40:04.340
Then he was out of the wealth of nations.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:08.130
And if he'd been around, he'd have coined
the phrase the Invisible Heart of Markets.

00:40:08.130 --> 00:40:09.590
&gt;&gt; That's wonderful.

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:13.840
So on the note of thinking the future and
getting our collective minds together,

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:17.180
we'll turn to the audience for
any questions you may have.

00:40:17.180 --> 00:40:20.259
So, Gracie and
Mariel will be rooming with the mics,

00:40:20.259 --> 00:40:23.520
so if you can just put up your hand,
and they'll come to you.

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:26.094
I think Charlie's got one at the back
there, straight off the bat.

00:40:28.113 --> 00:40:30.588
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; The mic will come to you in one second.

00:40:33.888 --> 00:40:36.100
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for the words.

00:40:36.100 --> 00:40:38.730
I did my undergrad at
Exeter College as well,

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:40.380
and so benefited from your support there.

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:40.970
&gt;&gt; Really?

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:42.940
&gt;&gt; So, thank you for that too.

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:45.690
My question was about
your political views,

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:49.900
and I guess based on your close
link to the Labour Party in the UK,

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:54.520
whether the advancement of impact
investing had changed those views?

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:58.560
A, in terms of what it means for
the role of the government, and

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:01.480
B, with how receptive different
political parties are to it.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.300
Great to meet somebody
who's an Exterian as I am.

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:09.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I just count all
the Brits just among the audience.

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:13.751
&gt;&gt; I owe a lot to ExeterCollege.

00:41:13.751 --> 00:41:20.310
You can imagine what it's like seven years
after coming to Britain as a refugee.

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:26.320
To walk into the quad of a college
founded in 1314 with illustrious

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:30.980
people who had gone there
before was a real privilege.

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:37.020
When I was a student at Oxford,
where I was very involved in politics.

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:40.140
I was president of the Oxford Union,
which was a debating society.

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:42.840
As a liberal,
I was a liberal for 30 years.

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:46.410
The liberals were in the center
between the Labour Party and

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:47.640
the Conservative Party.

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:50.750
When Tony Black came in, I became

00:41:50.750 --> 00:41:55.290
a Labour Party supporter because he
adopted the policies that I believed in.

00:41:56.390 --> 00:42:02.110
And when David Cameron came in,
the Cameron Government called me and

00:42:02.110 --> 00:42:05.230
said, will you come and
lead the social investments?

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:07.370
And another social investment task force.

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:10.580
Will you come to set up at
The Social Investment Bank

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:12.630
that you recommended in 2000.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.650
And if you do,

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:20.600
and you can put together an intelligent
plan for a Social Investment Bank

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:25.460
we will allow you to take all of
the unclaimed assets you asked for.

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:30.020
We have focused in 2005 asking for

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:35.120
government who was then trying
to take away bank accounts

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:40.040
that had been separated from their owners
for 15 years or more from the banks.

00:42:40.040 --> 00:42:41.700
The banks hold on to this money.

00:42:43.028 --> 00:42:48.370
And the Labour Government said to me,
we'll give you 75 million pounds,

00:42:48.370 --> 00:42:53.410
rather than the 250 million pounds you
were asking for as equity for this bank.

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:56.450
We said, well we start with a couple
million pounds of philanthropic

00:42:56.450 --> 00:43:00.500
money while we think about it, because 75
million pounds ain't a lot of money in

00:43:00.500 --> 00:43:04.365
financial terms when you're dealing with
the social issues we're talking about.

00:43:04.365 --> 00:43:10.300
A cut a long story short, the Conservative
Government allowed us to start the first

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:15.210
Social Investment Bank, independent of
government, with just one government

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:21.360
representative at the trust level
which looks to keeping it on mission.

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:26.150
With a 600 million pounds,
a billion dollars of which 400

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:31.570
million pounds came from unclaimed assets,
and I found the Conservative Government

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:37.000
was completely sold on
the idea of the big society,

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:40.390
which was basically saying that you
have a big government that can't cope.

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:45.610
You need the society to do it, only we
didn't know how society could do it.

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:50.150
And the Social Investment Bank
was the first tangible

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:54.380
expression of what the big
society program really was.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:58.730
So, then I began to work very closely
with the Conservative Government.

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:03.750
And I like to say that I'm about
policy rather than politics, and

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:09.200
this issue of impact investment whether
it's in the United States where the two

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:13.030
social impact bombs were launched in the
democratic state and the republican state.

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:20.470
Or whether it's in the UK, we've
managed to keep it a bipartisan issue.

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:26.030
To stress for those who like market forces
and for those who, on the other hand,

00:44:26.030 --> 00:44:30.490
feel the state should be responsible for
all of these things that this does both.

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:36.630
It improves government, and
it bring entrepreneurship and capital and

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:39.750
market forces at attacking social issues.

00:44:41.850 --> 00:44:45.440
&gt;&gt; Since Dave is braze nose
chap from Oxford, so am I,

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.590
I'm happy you gave him a shout out.

00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:50.353
Next question.

00:44:50.353 --> 00:44:52.130
So, yeah.

00:44:52.130 --> 00:44:54.900
&gt;&gt; Okay.
Hi, my name is Mario, I'm Mexican.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:45:00.810
How do you think about the line that
divides what is impact investing,

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:02.740
and what is not?

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:06.170
To me, any business that makes profit,

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:11.111
is creating jobs, is creating innovation,
and advancing society.

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:16.990
Many things could be argued against
tobacco, alcohol, what not.

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.620
Yeah, how do you think about that line?

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:23.610
&gt;&gt; So, there's a big distinction
we made in the G8 Task Force

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:28.710
report between investment with impact and
impact investment.

00:45:28.710 --> 00:45:30.956
We are all in favor of
investment with impact.

00:45:30.956 --> 00:45:38.740
Businesses that's create jobs,
improve lives in their communities.

00:45:38.740 --> 00:45:41.560
But that's not going to deal with
social issues in the way we need.

00:45:42.650 --> 00:45:48.500
Impact investment is investments where you
have measurable social or environmental

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:53.300
goals set, and you measure their
achievement alongside financial return.

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:59.170
And that is going to create the innovation
that will lead to a revolution.

00:46:00.530 --> 00:46:05.720
And so we see impact investment
sitting between philanthropy

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:11.670
on the right-hand side which will begin
to measure much more as it gives grants.

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:16.150
But we'll see its own
business model disrupted.

00:46:16.150 --> 00:46:18.560
I'll talk about that in a moment.

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:26.660
And what we might call ESG, environmental
social governance, on the left hand side.

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:32.730
Or social responsible investment or SCR.

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:36.930
Where basically businesses
intend to do good but

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:40.890
don't set measurable objectives and
measure their achievement.

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:42.520
Intention is not enough.

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:48.850
We're living in a time when
intention has evolved, and

00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:53.220
the intention just to make
money is no longer sufficient.

00:46:53.220 --> 00:46:55.140
You need to do more than that.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:59.280
But the revolution is coming
by bringing the means

00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:03.290
that didn't exist before
to tackle social issues.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:08.870
And so I think what we're going to find
over time is that those businesses

00:47:08.870 --> 00:47:15.540
in the middle which are businesses
that set social and environmental,

00:47:15.540 --> 00:47:21.939
as well as financial objectives and
to those not-for-profits that innovate.

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:28.555
By creating measurable goals through
social impact bonds and in other ways,

00:47:28.555 --> 00:47:33.870
will bring the innovation required
to improve people's lives.

00:47:33.870 --> 00:47:37.460
And that will begin to
affect the businesses and

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:39.190
the philanthropists on the other side.

00:47:39.190 --> 00:47:44.997
One of the great parallels for me,
who lived through the Tech revolution and

00:47:44.997 --> 00:47:48.458
Silicon Valley has scored
most of the goals.

00:47:48.458 --> 00:47:53.636
But we invested in the company,
in that revolution,

00:47:53.636 --> 00:47:57.328
that cloned Dolly the sheep in the UK.

00:47:57.328 --> 00:48:02.217
It's one of the only companies out of
Europe that made it to the hundred most

00:48:02.217 --> 00:48:05.610
important companies of the 20th century.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:07.980
We lost all our money but,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:11.060
&gt;&gt; it was a great thing to do.

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:15.330
It's bringing a revolution in medicine.

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:21.468
I think we're going to find people now
who say I want to be the Microsoft

00:48:21.468 --> 00:48:25.530
of improving education.

00:48:25.530 --> 00:48:30.790
Or I want to be the Apple
of improving health, and

00:48:30.790 --> 00:48:34.280
I'm going to innovate in
the way that I do it.

00:48:35.510 --> 00:48:44.220
The disruption that is happening is right
across all the constituents of our world.

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:48.900
So when Jeff Riggs who was CEO
of the Gates Foundation says

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:53.530
the business model of philanthropy is
being disrupted, what does he mean?

00:48:53.530 --> 00:48:58.810
He means in the future philanthropists
are going to measure results for

00:48:58.810 --> 00:49:00.690
the grants program.

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:04.340
They're going to make commitments to
outcomes funds that pay out on social

00:49:04.340 --> 00:49:09.470
impact bonds and development impact bonds,
when the outcomes are reached, and

00:49:09.470 --> 00:49:13.510
they're going to invest from their
endowment a certain percentage.

00:49:13.510 --> 00:49:16.480
We're asking for 10% now.

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:21.780
In investment opportunities that have the
right balance of risk, return and impact.

00:49:22.820 --> 00:49:27.130
You look at the disruption to
the business model of entrepreneurs,

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:31.760
businesses are going to raise capital
on the basis of achieving financial and

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:34.290
social environmental goals.

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:39.290
You look at the disruption at the level
of social sector organizations.

00:49:39.290 --> 00:49:42.090
They're going to be able
to build a balance sheet.

00:49:42.090 --> 00:49:45.130
We already see six not for profit

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:49.920
service delivery providers in the the UK
with sales of more than 50 million pounds.

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:55.180
I don't know if you saw the figures
in the Dan Pallotta TED Talk, that in

00:49:55.180 --> 00:50:00.970
25 years in the States, 50,000 businesses
made it through 50 million of sales.

00:50:00.970 --> 00:50:05.120
And only 144 not for profits.

00:50:05.120 --> 00:50:10.400
So you have disruption coming to social
sector organizations that's been scaled

00:50:10.400 --> 00:50:12.410
and fund innovation.

00:50:12.410 --> 00:50:16.970
And you have disruption from governments
which begin to measure outcomes,

00:50:18.520 --> 00:50:21.940
instead of just paying for inputs.

00:50:21.940 --> 00:50:25.270
And which attract capital
to fund innovation

00:50:25.270 --> 00:50:28.390
from investors who take
the risk of failure,

00:50:28.390 --> 00:50:33.950
because they are motivated by
a blended financial and social return.

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:38.970
So for me, this impact revolution
is going to be as pervasive as

00:50:38.970 --> 00:50:44.880
the tech revolution has been, and
it's going to be led by the likes of you.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:48.150
You're the ones who
are going to innovate and

00:50:48.150 --> 00:50:53.570
you're the ones who are going to
scale this in such a way that we do

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:58.310
a better job of improving people's lives
than we've been able to do until now.

00:50:58.310 --> 00:51:01.880
&gt;&gt; So I think some of our professors
like Matt Ballack who teaches here and

00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:04.920
Heidi and Charles would be quite delighted
that you have just reenforced all of

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:08.480
the teachings they've been trying to
force on us the past couple of weeks.

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:13.155
&gt;&gt; Well Matt has been my partner,
one of my partners,

00:51:13.155 --> 00:51:18.160
very close partners in the task force and
now the global steering group

00:51:18.160 --> 00:51:23.470
of 13 countries plus the EU,
which has succeeded for Task Force.

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:26.840
&gt;&gt; So Sir Ronald, I get the privilege
of asking the last question.

00:51:26.840 --> 00:51:32.000
And as you alluded to earlier, you used to
be the President of the Oxford Union, so

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:33.570
back in the day you were in my seat,

00:51:33.570 --> 00:51:38.400
inviting the thought leaders to share
their thoughts, such as Bobby Kennedy.

00:51:38.400 --> 00:51:42.380
I was just wondering were there any pearls
of wisdom that particularly resonated and

00:51:42.380 --> 00:51:48.151
stayed with you, that you would
like to share with us today.

00:51:48.151 --> 00:51:51.860
&gt;&gt; I think it's right to be idealistic.

00:51:53.590 --> 00:51:59.840
I think when people say you shouldn't
be idealistic, they're really concerned

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:04.920
about their ability to be successful and
to be idealistic at the same time.

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:09.080
And I think the two
are totally consistent.

00:52:10.200 --> 00:52:15.410
I got into venture capital because
I thought it was socially good.

00:52:15.410 --> 00:52:20.900
Britain had 3 million unemployed, and
I thought it was a good thing to do.

00:52:20.900 --> 00:52:26.030
And it created a lot of jobs, and it
improved competitiveness of the country,

00:52:26.030 --> 00:52:28.830
and it improved the average
standard of living.

00:52:28.830 --> 00:52:31.790
But it really didn't bridge
the gaps between rich and poor.

00:52:31.790 --> 00:52:37.200
What we've discovered is that
two slogans of my generation.

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:42.900
Growth will cure economic ills and
the quality of opportunity,

00:52:42.900 --> 00:52:46.770
turned out to be empty slogans for
those whom society leaves behind.

00:52:48.160 --> 00:52:53.200
Economic growth creates an even
bigger gap between rich and poor.

00:52:54.582 --> 00:52:58.800
And equality of opportunity is
an empty phrase for somebody born into

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:02.480
a family where both parents
are unemployed and have a drug habit.

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:09.690
Unless we begin to focus on improving
the quality of our society,

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:11.730
our societies are going to break apart.

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:17.180
We already see major divides
occurring within our cities,

00:53:17.180 --> 00:53:21.150
within our countries, within regions.

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:27.120
And I think the zeitgeist today,
unlike the zeitgeist in 1977 to 69 when

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:32.400
I was where you are sitting now,
which is smallest

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:37.890
is going to be beautiful and innovation
is going to become from technology.

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:44.050
The zeitgeist today is being
impactful is going to be beautiful.

00:53:44.050 --> 00:53:47.410
And being impactful is going to
change our lives in the same way that

00:53:47.410 --> 00:53:48.970
technology has done.

00:53:48.970 --> 00:53:52.755
&gt;&gt; Well on that positive note,

00:53:52.755 --> 00:53:57.964
please join me in thanking Sir Ronald.

